## Project Overview

This started as a way to get better at Go by building something real instead of toy examples. The domain made sense given my background in cybersecurity certifications, and Docker security has enough complexity to force learning actual Go patterns. The result is a CLI that scans containers, images, daemon config, Dockerfiles, and compose files against CIS Docker Benchmark v1.6.0.

Installation is a single command:

```bash
go install github.com/CarterPerez-dev/docksec/cmd/docksec@latest
docksec scan
```

The tool lives in a separate repo specifically so `go install` works cleanly. Multiple output formats cover different workflows: colored terminal output for interactive use, JSON for scripting, SARIF for GitHub Security tab integration, and JUnit XML for CI test reporting.

## Analyzer Architecture

All target types implement the same interface, which keeps the scanner agnostic about what it's actually analyzing:

```go
type Analyzer interface {
    Name() string
    Analyze(ctx context.Context) (finding.Collection, error)
}
```

Five analyzers exist: ContainerAnalyzer inspects running containers via the Docker SDK, ImageAnalyzer checks local image configs, DaemonAnalyzer queries daemon settings, DockerfileAnalyzer parses Dockerfile instructions using BuildKit's parser, and ComposeAnalyzer handles docker-compose.yml files. Adding a new analyzer means implementing the interface and registering it in the scanner's build step. The orchestration code never touches analyzer internals.

## Concurrency Model

Scanning dozens of containers sequentially takes forever since each inspection round trips to the Docker daemon. The scanner uses errgroup with a worker pool and token bucket rate limiting:

```go
func (s *Scanner) runAnalyzers(ctx context.Context, analyzers []analyzer.Analyzer) (finding.Collection, error) {
    g, ctx := errgroup.WithContext(ctx)
    g.SetLimit(s.cfg.Workers)

    results := make(chan finding.Collection, len(analyzers))

    for _, a := range analyzers {
        a := a  // capture loop variable
        g.Go(func() error {
            if err := s.limiter.Wait(ctx); err != nil {
                return err
            }
            findings, err := a.Analyze(ctx)
            if err != nil {
                s.logger.Warn("analyzer failed", "name", a.Name(), "error", err)
                return nil
            }
            results <- findings
            return nil
        })
    }
    // ...
}
```

The `a := a` captures the loop variable for goroutine safety (Go 1.21 still needs this). Rate limiting at 50 requests per second with burst of 50 prevents overwhelming the Docker daemon. Even with 50 workers, API calls spread out over time instead of hammering all at once.

## Security Rules Engine

The rules packages contain the domain knowledge. Beyond the 40 Linux capabilities, there's a catalog of 200+ sensitive host paths with severity ratings and descriptions:

```go
var SensitiveHostPaths = map[string]PathInfo{
    "/var/run/docker.sock": {
        Severity:    finding.SeverityCritical,
        Description: "Docker daemon socket. Full control over Docker, container escape possible.",
    },
    "/etc/shadow": {
        Severity:    finding.SeverityCritical,
        Description: "Password hashes. Direct credential access.",
    },
    "/var/lib/kubelet": {
        Severity:    finding.SeverityCritical,
        Description: "Kubelet data. Kubernetes node secrets.",
    },
    // ... 200+ more paths across Docker, Kubernetes, cloud providers, CI runners
}
```

Path matching handles both exact matches and prefix checks, so mounting `/etc` catches everything under it. Secret detection uses 100+ regex patterns for AWS keys, GitHub tokens, Stripe keys, JWTs, private keys, and database connection strings. High entropy string detection catches random looking values that might be hardcoded secrets.

## CIS Benchmark Registry

Controls register themselves during init() and live in a central registry:

```go
func init() {
    registerContainerRuntimeControls()
    registerDaemonControls()
    // ...
}

func registerContainerRuntimeControls() {
    Register(Control{
        ID:          "5.4",
        Section:     "Container Runtime",
        Title:       "Ensure that privileged containers are not used",
        Severity:    finding.SeverityCritical,
        Description: "Privileged containers have all Linux kernel capabilities...",
        Remediation: "Do not run containers with --privileged flag...",
        Scored:      true,
        Level:       1,
    })
}
```

When an analyzer finds an issue, it looks up the control by ID to pull in the full metadata. This keeps rule definitions centralized instead of scattered across analyzer code. If CIS updates the benchmark, you change the registry once rather than hunting through every check function.

